  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
